{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 2540,
    "dDurationMs": 5020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Balancing a pencil or any other top heavy object on its tip is quiet precarious task"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 7680,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It's easy to see why top heavy objects are unstable"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 10480,
    "dDurationMs": 1660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They just...Wanna fall down"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12260,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Any little nudge will put it off balance"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with gravity happy to pull its center of mass even more off balance and the more off balance it is, the more gravity pulls until..."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21540,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Physicist call this kind of tipping an Inverted pendulum, becouse it´s kind of like upside down pendulum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 26540,
    "dDurationMs": 3860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And careful mathematical analysis of inverted pendulums will tell you the obvious:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 30400,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They're really unstable. In fact, these equations predict that if a perfectly balanced pencil with a perfectly sharp tip"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36360,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "were to get nudged off balance by just 1/10000th of the width of an atom,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40760,
    "dDurationMs": 2880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it would take only three seconds to fall over."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44020,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are indeed acrobats who can balance poles with other acrobats on top,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 47560,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and robots that can balance and catch and throw upright sticks,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 51320,
    "dDurationMs": 3640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and people who make seemingly impossible artistic rock stacks."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 54960,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in all these cases, keeping the objects balanced either requires an active stabilization effort,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 60460,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or else carefully taking advantage of the fact that the balancing point isn't actually a point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65020,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the question is this: can you balance a pencil sharpened to a perfect single atom wide tip"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 70080,
    "dDurationMs": 2600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on its tip, without active stabilization?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 72800,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, to start, you'd better get rid of all the air bumping off the sides of the pencil,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76040,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the kinetic energy of a typical oxygen molecule is enough to destabilize the pencil"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 80420,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Remember: if the pencil gets off-centered by even 1/10000th the width of an atom,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 84620,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it'll fall down in under three seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86720,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And you'd better cool the pencil and table down as close to absolute 0 as you can,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 91140,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "since the thermal jiggling of their atoms when they're hotter will similarly jiggle the pencil off balance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96280,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even then, matter at very small scales has a spread-out, wave-like nature"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 100460,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which means you can't have both the pencil perfectly upright"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103240,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and not moving at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105240,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And while this quantum effect is never a real life inhibitor of precarious pencil balancing,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109740,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it means you never even COULD succeed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 111940,
    "dDurationMs": 4100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, it's still fun to see how long you can keep a pencil balanced on your fingertip."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116040,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The shorter the pencil, the harder it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118300,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "My best time for a standard 19 cm pencil was 1,3 seconds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122560,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think you can beat it?"
    } ]
  } ]
}
